>From a design perspective that's largely the same, so okay.


> Well, I s'pose you have a point... but the question now is: which quest
> artifacts do you think should get spell resistance and which ones
> should stick with magic resistance?

Well if I had my druthers I'd supply spell resistance with a ring
instead of an amulet, but that's just off thet top of my head.

I'd also like a little more information on which effects of vanilla
magic resistance got transferred over to spell resistance.  (There are
quite a few: protection from a number of traps, fewer item cursings,
protection from many spells including death and destroy armor, and
protection from outside polymorph, among others.  Magic resistance is
perhaps the most valuable intrinsic in vanilla Nethack in the late
game, and it's still mighty useful early on because of traps in the
mines and gnomes with wands.)


> >*He* already has one -- that's what players are trying to plan against!
> > What you want to give *him* is a Spell of Cheap Death -- without
> >defense.
>
> There is a defense; it's called "not letting her get near you." A
> potential demigod shouldn't find that task to be too costly.

This is not always possible on Astral, which is no teleport, teleport
rarely works on him, and is full of angels.  Avoiding sources of
instadeath brings up what I'm going to suddenly call a "Demogorgon
situation:" instances where the game comes down to making certain
specific moves to avoid immanent death.  Get sickened by Daddy-D?  You
need to remedy that *immediately*.  Cockatrice turning you to stone?
Get that fixed, pronto!  If Death has an instant kill attack that
cannot be overcome, then Death becoming adjacent to you would be such a
situation, and there are not a lot a ways out of it.

I'm not saying it's not overcomeable, but the solution would become
more about making sure Death doesn't get there in the first place, and
if he does, to having a means of stepping away.  While that could
actually be very interesting (cockatrices are interesting monsters
too), but if one wants Death to retain parity with the other riders,
it's probably not a good idea.

I do like the idea of making the riders a bit more dangerous, but Death
is already the most dangerous of the three, since he can sap your
maximum HP without defense.


